%I A363097 #16 Sep 30 2026 00:14:11
%S A363097 1,2,3,5,8,9,12,11,18,15,18,17,28,25,34,31,46,39,42,31,50,41,62,53,76,
%T A363097 61,86,69,72,53,82,71,102,65,82,75,76,73,110,79,118,99,102,75,84,69,
%U A363097 90,71,118,107,156,99,112,101,154,115,144,105,106,111,132,101,162,117,136
%N A363097 a(0) = 1, a(n) = n + phi(a(n-1)), where phi is Euler totient function.
%C A363097 Conjecture: 1.25*n < a(n) < 4*n for n > 0.
%C A363097 From _Robert Israel_, Sep 29 2026: (Start)
%C A363097 The upper bound is true. Indeed a(n) < 4*n for even n > 0 and a(n) < 3*n for odd n.
%C A363097 The proof is by induction on n.
%C A363097 Note that phi(k) <= k/2 if k is even, and a(n) == n (mod 2) for n > 2.
%C A363097 If n > 2 is even with a(n) < 4*n, then a(n+1) = n+1 + phi(a(n)) <= n+1 + a(n)/2 < n + 1 + 2*n < 3*(n+1), and a(n+2) = n+2 + phi(a(n+1)) < n+2 + 3*(n+1) < 4*(n+2).
%C A363097 On the other hand, the lower bound seems unlikely to be true.
%C A363097 For large enough K, the product P of the first K primes has phi(P) < P/16, and phi(t) < t/16 for all multiples of P.
%C A363097 If a(n-1) is ever a multiple of P, then a(n) < n + a(n-1)/16 < n + (n-1)/4 < 1.25*n. (End)
%H A363097 Robert Israel, <a href="/A363097/b363097.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%e A363097 a(5) = 9 because a(4) = 8 and 5 + phi(a(4)) = 5 + 4 = 9.
%p A363097 a:= proc(n) option remember;
%p A363097      `if`(n=0, 1, n+numtheory[phi](a(n-1)))
%p A363097     end:
%p A363097 seq(a(n), n=0..100);  # _Alois P. Heinz_, May 20 2023
%t A363097 a[0]=1; a[n_]:=a[n]=n+EulerPhi[a[n-1]]; Array[a,65,0]
%Y A363097 Cf. A000010.
%K A363097 nonn,changed
%O A363097 0,2
%A A363097 _Giorgos Kalogeropoulos_, May 19 2023
